# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to
# CRLF on checkout. That is fine for files Windows consumes and actively
# harmful for files Linux does:
#
#   - a shell script picks up a CR on its shebang line and fails to run at
#     all, with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M"
#   - a Dockerfile carries the CR into RUN commands
#   - .env.example copied to a real .env puts a trailing CR inside every
#     environment variable's value, which is invisible in most output and
#     breaks secret comparison and host names
#
# So pin the ones that cross over to a Linux host, and pin the Windows-only
# ones the other way rather than leaving them to the global setting.

* text=auto

# Runs on Linux — must stay LF.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# Windows-only.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj       text eol=crlf
*.csproj        text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch.
*.usmap         binary
*.pak           binary
*.dll           binary
*.exe           binary
*.pdb           binary
*.bin           binary
